B.IO=new A.n9("Core accretion","Standard model (planet formation theory)","Dust \u2192 pebbles \u2192 planetesimals \u2192 planetary embryos. Gas giants form when a solid core grows fast enough to capture hydrogen and helium before the disk disperses.","Matches the architecture of the Solar System, exoplanet statistics, and the inferred timescales from meteorite isotopes.","Struggles with the formation speed of Jupiter and with the known population of close-in super-Earths.")
B.IP=new A.n9("Disk instability","Alternative rapid-formation model","Parts of a massive, cold disk collapse directly into giant planets through gravity, like a miniature star formation event.","Could explain giant planets at large separations and some directly imaged exoplanets.","Requires very massive, cold disks; most disks observed so far do not qualify.")
B.IM=new A.n9("Giant impact (Theia)","Moon-formation model","A Mars-sized body collided with proto-Earth about 4.5 billion years ago. The debris disk formed the Moon and re-melted Earth.","Lunar and terrestrial rocks share oxygen, titanium and tungsten isotopes; the Moon has a small iron core and volatile depletion.","The details of the impactor\u2019s size, angle and isotopic mixing are still debated.")
B.IL=new A.n9("Late veneer","Final accretion phase","After core formation, a late wave of chondritic impacts added a thin layer of material \u2014 including gold, platinum and iridium \u2014 to Earth\u2019s mantle.","Earth\u2019s mantle has more highly siderophile elements than core formation alone predicts, matching chondritic ratios.","The mass and timing of the veneer, and whether it was one event or many, remain uncertain.")
B.IN=new A.n9("Water delivery","Ocean-origin debate","Water arrived as ice-rich planetesimals from beyond the snow line, or was already bound in the rocks that built Earth.","D/H ratios in Earth\u2019s oceans match carbonaceous chondrites and some comets; water is seen in protoplanetary disks.","No single source fits every isotope measurement; multiple reservoirs are likely.")
